7. Cross-Cutting Governance

The following concepts apply across all architectural layers:

Concept	Responsibility
Engineering Context	Defines operating conditions
Engineering Intent	Defines objectives
Engineering Constraints	Defines permissible boundaries
Engineering Confidence	Qualifies certainty
Engineering Traceability	Maintains provenance
Engineering Explainability	Provides justification

These concepts SHALL be available to every layer and SHALL NOT be confined to a single architectural component.

8. High-Level Information Flow

The canonical information flow is:

Engineering Reality
        │
Engineering Observation
        │
Engineering Evidence
        │
Engineering Understanding
        │
Engineering Memory
        │
Engineering Knowledge
        │
Engineering Reasoning
        │
Engineering Capability
        │
Decision Support

Each stage transforms or enriches information while preserving traceability to preceding stages.

9. Architectural Boundaries

The Atlas architecture distinguishes between:

External Environment: engineering reality, users, external systems, sensors, data sources.
Atlas Core: cognitive processing governed by ACC and AHS.
Integration Interfaces: APIs, connectors, import/export services, and messaging mechanisms.

This separation ensures that external changes do not alter the canonical cognitive architecture.

10. Conformance

An implementation conforms to this specification if it:

Preserves the canonical layer structure.
Implements responsibilities consistent with ACC.
Maintains defined information flow.
Supports the required cross-cutting governance concepts.
Does not introduce architectural shortcuts that violate layer responsibilities.
11. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Additional specialized layers.
Distributed execution models.
Multi-agent coordination architectures.
Domain-specific cognitive extensions.

Such additions SHALL preserve the canonical layer responsibilities defined by this specification.